  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Quiet weather period with warming trend next\nweek.\n\nOvernight cloud cover across the mountains will hold fast over\nsoutheast West Virginia, but show some variation in coverage\nover southwest Virginia and the High Country of North Carolina.\nA small burst of light snow showers/flurries is still possible\nover portions of southeast West Virginia later tonight, but as\nexpected earlier, little to no accumulation is expected.\n\nGoing into next week a column of high pressure systems will \ndominate the eastern half of the United States with a center of \na surface high over the Mid-Atlantic. This will result in mostly\nclear skies and calm winds for the majority of next week. The \nother real change in the weather pattern will be at the 500mb \nlevel where a Rex Block temporarily forms over the southeastern \nCONUS before the high component is squeezed out from a trough \nthat passes to our north later in the week. The presence of the \nhigh, however, will result in increasing heights and a warming \ntrend that will provide some abnormally warm temperatures for \nthis time of year. For perspective, the highs for Sunday will be\nin the lower 40s to upper 50s and then in the 60s to lower 70s \nby Thursday. With the clear skies and calm winds, temperatures \nwill cool off considerably each night with some lows in the 20s \nand gradually \"warming\" into the 30s.",
